# Default: every file git detects as text checks out LF (2026-08-13, with
# .editorconfig — kills the CRLF-vs-LF churn between VS Code on Windows and
# the tools that read the working tree). `auto` keeps git's binary detection,
# so images and other binaries are untouched; explicit rules below still win.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must use LF — they run on Linux schedulers (Control-M / cron);
# a CRLF in the shebang breaks execution.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.ksh text eol=lf

# Python must use LF in the WORKING TREE too, not just in the index (J10 stage 0).
# Git already stores LF via autocrlf, so this changes no committed bytes — it
# changes what lands on disk at checkout. Needed because [tool.ruff.format]
# pins line-ending = "lf": without this rule, a Windows checkout materializes
# CRLF, `ruff format --check` reports every file as needing reformat purely on
# line endings, and stage 5's "exits 0" acceptance is unreachable on that
# machine while passing on the Linux runner. Same lesson as the render-ordering
# bug (tests/unit/test_render_determinism.py): make the platforms agree rather
# than picking one to be right.
*.py text eol=lf

# Binary documents — never eol-normalize (autocrlf=true would corrupt them).
*.pdf binary
